## Steps to reproduce

Attempt connection to an IC-705 (or other modern Icom radio) using the incorrect serial port. (For example, on my MacBook Pro, `/dev/cu.usbmodem143201` is the CI-V port and `/dev/cu.usbmodem143203` is for GPS; I used the latter for this test.) Additionally, set timeouts as follows:

```
rig_set_conf(rig_, rig_token_lookup(rig_, "timeout"), "1000");
rig_set_conf(rig_, rig_token_lookup(rig_, "retry"), "1");
rig_set_conf(rig_, rig_token_lookup(rig_, "timeout_retry"), "1");
```

## Expected behavior

After 1 second, `rig_open()` returns `RIG_ETIMEOUT`.

## Actual behavior

`rig_open()` does not return (at least after ~30s or so when I pushed Ctrl-C).

## Notes

I see a lot of `icom_one_transaction: unknown async?  read again` messages in the terminal window, which seems to be printed by code in `rigs/icom/frame.c`:

```c
    if (sendbuf[3] != buf[2])
    {
        rig_debug(RIG_DEBUG_VERBOSE, "%s: unknown async?  read again\n", __func__);
        hl_usleep(100);
        rig_flush(rp);
        goto collision_retry;
    }
```

`collision_retry` itself doesn't seem to track the number of retries except in the case when `COL` gets returned by a device on the CI-V bus, so we never get bounced back to the higher level timeout/retry checking logic (and thus the settings set by `rig_set_conf` don't seem to be getting used).

AC log rig uses the Dummy rig and aclog.c translates the AC Log commands to hamlib commands for this virtual rig interface.

When this hamlib rig interface receives frequencies with commas in them, 1,296.100Mhz for example, the parsing stops at the comma and thus only the GHz placed digit is extracted. There is a comma for all frequencies 1GHz and higher.  

Here is what is received from AC Log for 1296.1711MHz from the WSJT-X Rig Control log.

`[2025-04-17 16:32:23.578614][00:00:02.941668][RIGCTRL:trace] read_transaction: string='<CMD><READBMFRESPONSE><BAND>23</BAND><MODE>SSB</MODE><MODETEST>PH</MODETEST><FREQ>1,296.171100</FREQ></CMD>
'
`

In aclog_get_freq() function, the frequency is read from the http string.  
<FREQ>1,296.171100</FREQ>

The comma is a delimiter and ends up with just the GHz digit.  That digit is then multiplied by * 1e6 converting MHz to Hz resulting in a frequency = 1.000MHz.  Same goes for 2304 getting 2MHz and 5760 getting 5Mhz.  Stripping the comma and other whitespace before multiplying should resolve the problem.

Here is the relevant code excerpt from aclog_get_freq().
See the sscanf line.   I suggest whitespace and comma (not decimal point) be stripped before the sscanf.

``` c
char *p = strstr(value, "<FREQ>");
*freq = 0;

if (p) { sscanf(p, "<FREQ>%lf", freq); }

*freq *= 1e6; // convert from MHz to Hz

